{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Exer4gEWa1d263PfMMhPZx","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Challenger disaster February 1986 how many astronauts died\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Challenger Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost\",\"url\":\"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Explosion - Date, Astronauts & Shuttle\",\"url\":\"<redacted> in spaceflight\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1986_in_spaceflight\"},{\"title\":\"The space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Jarvis\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Jarvis\"},{\"title\":\"The Crew Members Who Died in the Challenger Disaster\",\"url\":\"<redacted> astronauts were killed in the 1986 space shuttle ...\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 28, 2016\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, the Challenger disaster occurred on January 28, 1986, when the space shuttle Challenger tragically exploded shortly after launch, resulting in the deaths of all seven crew members.\n\nNote: The disaster occurred in January 1986, not February 1986 as mentioned in your query.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebea0fd8-3649-4022-931b-0ad20974ccc7","uuid":"9d269ad0-d6a7-4f5d-a325-cb5bab591395","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:12:42.676Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Challenger disaster February 1986 how many astronauts died","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01TSjZN9eeS6H5Qq2t9ESjSZ","content":[{"title":"Challenger Accident | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost","url":"<redacted> disaster | Summary, Date, Cause, & Facts | Britannica","url":"<redacted> Explosion - Date, Astronauts & Shuttle","url":"<redacted> in spaceflight","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/1986_in_spaceflight"},{"title":"The space shuttle Challenger explodes after liftoff | January 28, 1986 | HISTORY","url":"<redacted> Jarvis","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gregory_Jarvis"},{"title":"The Crew Members Who Died in the Challenger Disaster","url":"<redacted> astronauts were killed in the 1986 space shuttle ...","url":"<redacted> 28, 2016","url":"<redacted> on the search results, the Challenger disaster occurred on January 28, 1986, when the space shuttle Challenger tragically exploded shortly after launch, resulting in the deaths of all seven crew members.\n\nNote: The disaster occurred in January 1986, not February 1986 as mentioned in your query."],"durationSeconds":2.758327171000001,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_015gGN1myrp8DPYRAuWLBUFv","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 1 Uranus flyby encounter\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager 2's close encounter with Uranus wasn't in the plan\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Journey: 30 Years Since Voyager 2’s Encounter With Uranus (Part 1) - AmericaSpace\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2: The First Uranus Flyby | Drew Ex Machina\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Mission Celebrates 30 Years Since Uranus | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 1 and 2 Explore the Outer Planets | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost\",\"url\":\"<redacted> to the Uranus Data Archive Page\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 1980, Voyager 1 flew close enough to Titan to reveal its thick nitrogen atmosphere — but the gravity-assist geometry bent the spacecraft permanently out of the planetary plane, trading Uranus and Neptune for one close look at Saturn’s largest moon\",\"url\":\"<redacted> voyager encounter with uranus\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 1: URANUS ENCOUNTER\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, I can clarify an important point about your query:\n\nVoyager 2 reached Uranus in January 1986 and Neptune in August 1989, becoming the only spacecraft yet to visit either planet. **Voyager 1 did not perform a Uranus flyby encounter.**\n\nHere's why: Voyager 1 performing a successful flyby of Saturn's moon Titan meant that Voyager 2 could continue on the Grand Tour, taking in Uranus and Neptune. Following their encounter with Saturn, Voyager 1 left the plane of the ecliptic (the plane in which Earth orbits the Sun) while Voyager 2 remained within it to travel to Uranus and Neptune.\n\nThe Voyager 2 Uranus encounter was significant. Uranus Flyby Visual images were obtained from 1985-11-06 to 1986-02-19 with closest approach of the Voyager 2 spacecraft on Jan 24, 1986 coming within 81,500 km of the cloudtops. The flyby produced a tremendous amount of data about Uranus – the planet had a magnetic field that was not aligned with its rotational axis. Additional rings appeared in Voyager 2's data, and images of the moon Miranda showed signs consistent with a violent impact that may have blown it apart and allowed it to reform.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebea0fd8-3649-4022-931b-0ad20974ccc7","uuid":"8938a94a-9131-483a-a817-b572cfb64690","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:12:47.185Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 1 Uranus flyby encounter","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UssuDMxs1b3oLcdKJR6PG4","content":[{"title":"Voyager 2's close encounter with Uranus wasn't in the plan","url":"<redacted> Journey: 30 Years Since Voyager 2’s Encounter With Uranus (Part 1) - AmericaSpace","url":"<redacted> 2: The First Uranus Flyby | Drew Ex Machina","url":"<redacted> Mission Celebrates 30 Years Since Uranus | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)","url":"<redacted> 1 and 2 Explore the Outer Planets | History | Research Starters | EBSCOhost","url":"<redacted> to the Uranus Data Archive Page","url":"<redacted> 1980, Voyager 1 flew close enough to Titan to reveal its thick nitrogen atmosphere — but the gravity-assist geometry bent the spacecraft permanently out of the planetary plane, trading Uranus and Neptune for one close look at Saturn’s largest moon","url":"<redacted> voyager encounter with uranus","url":"<redacted> 1: URANUS ENCOUNTER","url":"<redacted> on the search results, I can clarify an important point about your query:\n\nVoyager 2 reached Uranus in January 1986 and Neptune in August 1989, becoming the only spacecraft yet to visit either planet. **Voyager 1 did not perform a Uranus flyby encounter.**\n\nHere's why: Voyager 1 performing a successful flyby of Saturn's moon Titan meant that Voyager 2 could continue on the Grand Tour, taking in Uranus and Neptune. Following their encounter with Saturn, Voyager 1 left the plane of the ecliptic (the plane in which Earth orbits the Sun) while Voyager 2 remained within it to travel to Uranus and Neptune.\n\nThe Voyager 2 Uranus encounter was significant. Uranus Flyby Visual images were obtained from 1985-11-06 to 1986-02-19 with closest approach of the Voyager 2 spacecraft on Jan 24, 1986 coming within 81,500 km of the cloudtops. The flyby produced a tremendous amount of data about Uranus – the planet had a magnetic field that was not aligned with its rotational axis. Additional rings appeared in Voyager 2's data, and images of the moon Miranda showed signs consistent with a violent impact that may have blown it apart and allowed it to reform."],"durationSeconds":6.961535572000001,"searchCount":1}}
