{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01ENKjCLc6pijcZeNL2mkyZ3","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Miranda encounter distance 17560 miles kilometers\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science\",\"url\":\"<redacted> A Moon of Uranus\",\"url\":\"<redacted> 2's Miranda image catalog | The Planetary Society\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Apart at Birth: 70 Years Since the Discovery of Miranda, Mysterious Moon Viewed by Voyager 2 - AmericaSpace\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Satellite - Miranda | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Moon: The Solar System’s Tallest Cliff - Astronive\",\"url\":\"<redacted> - Voyager 2\",\"url\":\"<redacted> - Voyager 2\",\"url\":\"<redacted> Searches\",\"url\":\"<redacted> on the search results, I found information about Voyager 2's Miranda encounter:\n\nIn flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), Voyager 2 came closest to any object in its nearly decade-long travels at that point.\n\nThe search results also provide additional context: Due to the spacecraft's inbound trajectory, it passed closer to Miranda—about 18,000 miles (29,000 km)—than any of the other moons. This slightly different figure likely represents a different measurement point in the encounter sequence. The close approach occurred during Voyager 2's January 24, 1986 flyby.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebea0fd8-3649-4022-931b-0ad20974ccc7","uuid":"e255798f-6d35-416b-add5-cee3566d451b","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:12:54.867Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Miranda encounter distance 17560 miles kilometers","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01LViH7EdR5pu8rfKRauGqGK","content":[{"title":"Voyager 2: First To Visit All Four Giants — NASA Science","url":"<redacted> A Moon of Uranus","url":"<redacted> 2's Miranda image catalog | The Planetary Society","url":"<redacted> Apart at Birth: 70 Years Since the Discovery of Miranda, Mysterious Moon Viewed by Voyager 2 - AmericaSpace","url":"<redacted> Satellite - Miranda | NASA Jet Propulsion Laboratory (JPL)","url":"<redacted> Moon: The Solar System’s Tallest Cliff - Astronive","url":"<redacted> - Voyager 2","url":"<redacted> - Voyager 2","url":"<redacted> Searches","url":"<redacted> on the search results, I found information about Voyager 2's Miranda encounter:\n\nIn flying by Miranda at a range of only 17,560 miles (28,260 kilometers), Voyager 2 came closest to any object in its nearly decade-long travels at that point.\n\nThe search results also provide additional context: Due to the spacecraft's inbound trajectory, it passed closer to Miranda—about 18,000 miles (29,000 km)—than any of the other moons. This slightly different figure likely represents a different measurement point in the encounter sequence. The close approach occurred during Voyager 2's January 24, 1986 flyby."],"durationSeconds":4.095371471000006,"searchCount":1}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X7GVMKcUUGTvtEbLYX7sTm","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"Voyager 2 Uranus wind speeds 450 km/h discovery\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Uranus: A Haywire Magnetosphere During Voyager 2 Flyby | Max Planck Institute for Solar System Research\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"How the Mighty Winds of Uranus and Neptune Blow | Space\",\"url\":\"<redacted> (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Bianca (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bianca_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Ophelia (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Portia (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portia_(moon)\"},{\"title\":\"Umbriel\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umbriel\"},{\"title\":\"Miranda (moon)\",\"url\":\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)\"}]\n\nBased on the search results, I found information about Uranus's wind speeds from Voyager 2 observations, though the specific value mentioned is higher than 450 km/h.\n\nUranus has a peak wind speed of 900 km/h (560 mph), which represents the strongest winds measured on the planet. The winds of Uranus can blow clouds up to 560 miles per hour (900 kilometers per hour), and the researchers investigated the gravity fields of those worlds using data gathered by NASA's Voyager 2 spacecraft and ground-based telescopes.\n\nAdditionally, the scientists discovered the winds blow in relatively thin weather layers no more than 600 miles (1,000 kilometers) deep on both planets.\n\nSo while your search query mentioned 450 km/h, the actual peak wind speeds discovered by Voyager 2 observations of Uranus are approximately 900 km/h.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ebea0fd8-3649-4022-931b-0ad20974ccc7","uuid":"3ae01d67-fb30-452f-8094-2610b080802c","timestamp":"2026-09-26T17:12:55.479Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"Voyager 2 Uranus wind speeds 450 km/h discovery","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01XLJKNJMPHBZr8gEwzRASgX","content":[{"title":"Uranus: A Haywire Magnetosphere During Voyager 2 Flyby | Max Planck Institute for Solar System Research","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Puck_(moon)"},{"title":"How the Mighty Winds of Uranus and Neptune Blow | Space","url":"<redacted> (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Oberon_(moon)"},{"title":"Bianca (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bianca_(moon)"},{"title":"Ophelia (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ophelia_(moon)"},{"title":"Portia (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Portia_(moon)"},{"title":"Umbriel","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Umbriel"},{"title":"Miranda (moon)","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Miranda_(moon)"}]},"Based on the search results, I found information about Uranus's wind speeds from Voyager 2 observations, though the specific value mentioned is higher than 450 km/h.\n\nUranus has a peak wind speed of 900 km/h (560 mph), which represents the strongest winds measured on the planet. The winds of Uranus can blow clouds up to 560 miles per hour (900 kilometers per hour), and the researchers investigated the gravity fields of those worlds using data gathered by NASA's Voyager 2 spacecraft and ground-based telescopes.\n\nAdditionally, the scientists discovered the winds blow in relatively thin weather layers no more than 600 miles (1,000 kilometers) deep on both planets.\n\nSo while your search query mentioned 450 km/h, the actual peak wind speeds discovered by Voyager 2 observations of Uranus are approximately 900 km/h."],"durationSeconds":5.202313796000002,"searchCount":1}}
